very confidential and wanted to avoid being seen during the mailing process\u2026\n[3540] \u2013 Alright, let's all say it together! [3541] Then, Sagihara could have called! Pressing \"0\" on the hotel phone would have allowed him to call without going through the boss lady. If necessary, he could have discussed any secrets! And there's more! [3542] What if the recipient's house didn't have a phone? This is also unlikely, as the owner of Fukuyama House said, \"The telephone penetration rate in the village is 100%.\" Therefore, the idea that Sagihara wrote the second letter and did not send it is also incorrect. If it was not sent, how did he dispose of it? Did he personally deliver it to someone? Or perhaps... Sagihara wrote the second letter but tore it up and discarded it because he changed his mind? No, it should not be, as if it was torn up, he would have asked the boss lady for another sheet of paper. Since it was not torn up, Sagihara must have changed his mind and crumpled it up. We need to search through the trash to see what he wrote on it \u2013 this theory should hold up, right? For me, it does. [3543] \"Hey, what are you thinking about? Yukiawa Sagawa?\" The one sitting across from me, Weitaka, stretched out his arm to pour me a drink. [3544] I picked up the glass, \"I was thinking about the mail...\"\n[3545] After taking a sip of beer, I told him all my thoughts. I only mentioned the conclusion, as beer can become tasteless when spoken in detail. [3546] \"How did you come to this conclusion?\" The leader of the study group teased me with narrowed eyes. Okay, you're clearly trying to make my beer tasteless! [3547] \"Shouji wrote another letter, but later changed his mind, so he crumpled it...\" I invited everyone to join me on the long journey to the conclusion I reached. [3548] \"So Aoki was silent because he was thinking about these things?\" Miyuki said absent-mindedly, and the tone of her voice did not sound respectful at all. [3549] \"Aoki, I want you to help me push this forward.\" Since the topic had been opened, Wakamatsu's interest seemed to have been aroused, \"What was the reason for Shouji changing his mind?\" [3550] \"I don't know. People... often change their minds. Maybe he wrote a love letter and later felt embarrassed...\"\n[3551] I didn't think of that! [3552] However, just this point alone, it may be like the instant response I gave earlier, and thinking about it won't make it clear. It seems that the train I breathlessly took has reached its destination! [3553] If Kuroda Sensei were here, what kind of reasoning would he present? Miyaya once joked that Sensei was \"Akin of the Detective Fiction Research Club,\" would he start with a line like \"The photographer changed his mind and threw away the letter\" and then proceed to think? Where would he find the breakthrough point? I don't know. [3554] \"Speaking of emails\u2026\" Yuri placed the empty bottle under the table, \"I heard that the police are investigating the alibi of Roomi-san in the interrogation room! I heard this in the evening when I went to withdraw money. He was in the office when I arrived and peeked out the window, saying, 'Sir, I give up.'\" [3555] Roomi arrived at Fukuju at 9 o'clock and drank with us afterwards, which is a very solid alibi. However, the problem is where he was and what he was doing before he arrived at Fukuju. According to Yuri, Roomi went home for a short nap after leaving work at 5 o'clock and woke up around 7:30. He ate something and then watched TV until he suddenly wanted to drink, so he decided to go to Fukuju. Although he thought it was impossible to succeed that night, he found out that the store was still open. He and Yuri were both alone in their actions and could not be verified by anyone. When he said, \"Sir, I give up,\" Yuri probably responded with a wry smile, \"So do I.\" [3556] \"Oh, right, right! Speaking of Roomwood-san\u2026\"\n[3557] Yurihara has a habit of changing the topic while saying \