ad a hat on his head. Although his face was not clear, it was known that he was elderly, and his right hand was holding a cane. The old man's appearance was somewhat similar to Roomwood's face. [3561] \"Yes,\" Yuri replies. [3562] \"I think Mr. Shizu really wants to build such a palace!\" [3563] This is my inner thought. [3564] \"I don't remember hearing Mr. Shizu express any specific impression about this building. I don't think he borrowed the dream of Sawaragi wholesale. I remember the first time I showed him this book. 'There's a strange mailman in France!' I showed him the book as if it were an appetizer, and he stared at it intently. His enthusiasm was so great that I was a little afraid, as he didn't even hear me say, 'You seem to like it.' I was surprised that something I casually handed him had such an impact on him, so I gave him the book. This is a copy I bought later.\" [3565] \"I have seen Mr. Shizu bring this book to his office. Perhaps he considers it a talisman.\" Miyuki says. I wonder if she has followed the conversation. [3566] \"Do you know Mr. Shima's dream?\" I asked unexpectedly. Because Mr. Shima seemed quiet and not good at socializing, it was hard to imagine that Miyuki would know his dreams. [3567] \"I heard it when I was leaving town and taking a ride with Mr. Shima. He wasn't just passing the time when he told me, but I asked him first, 'Is holding a book of architecture your hobby?' He answered, 'There are photos of buildings I like in it.' His answer piqued my interest, so I asked, 'Is it a woman you like?' He replied seriously, 'No.' I responded, 'Then what is it?' In the conversation, we talked about his ideal palace. I asked him then, 'What kind of palace is it? Can I see it?' A few days later, when I was going to send a package, he really showed it to me. He flipped to the page of the ideal palace, 'This is the building I mentioned.' Perhaps because he often flipped through that page, even if the book was lying around, it would automatically open to that page, and I remember there were many fingerprints on it!\" [3568] \"What are you talking about?\" Wakatsuki asked, unable to follow the conversation. [3569] \"Oh, I'm sorry. It's like this...\"\n[3570] Fujisawa briefly explained the situation, and Wakatsuki handed the book to him. Wakatsuki put on his glasses and slowly read the description of the ideal palace. [3571] \"What is Disneyland?\" This time, Yuzu asked, and Mei also tilted her head slightly. [3572] Wakatsuki seemed to feel that he had talked too much, and was momentarily speechless, \"Actually, it's about the village of Mokumura. That is... someone wants to open the village so that the various artworks there can be appreciated by ordinary people... However, I call it an art Disneyland...\"\n[3573] Wakatsuki omitted the situation that Mokumura was facing. Perhaps he didn't want to casually mention the troubles of his acquaintances. [3574] \"It's an art Disneyland, but you just said it was a Disneyland that combines art and natural wonders?\" Mei asked again. [3575] Wakatsuki omitted the situation that Mokumura was facing. Perhaps he didn't want to casually mention the troubles of his acquaintances.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."